# Duration, not a legacy Long

`delay`, `withTimeout`, `withTimeoutOrNull`, `debounce`, `sample`, and `timeout` each have a `kotlin.time.Duration` overload. Pass that. The Long milliseconds overload is the one Android Studio flags as "Legacy Long overload can be converted to Duration".

Pass a `Duration`. Import `kotlin.time.Duration.Companion.milliseconds` (or `.seconds`).

```kotlin
delay(PREVIEW_MS.milliseconds)
withTimeout(COMMAND_TIMEOUT_MS.milliseconds) { response.await() }
states.debounce(STATE_DEBOUNCE_MS.milliseconds)
```

Leave the Long overload unused:

```kotlin
delay(PREVIEW_MS)
withTimeout(COMMAND_TIMEOUT_MS) { response.await() }
states.debounce(STATE_DEBOUNCE_MS)
```

Import `kotlin.time.Duration.Companion.milliseconds` (or `.seconds`). A computed count converts the same way: `delay((minutes * MILLISECONDS_PER_MINUTE).milliseconds)`.

A file-top timeout that is itself a `Duration` is a `val`, not a `const val`, and its name is still `UPPER_SNAKE` (`kotlin-detekt-top-level-property-naming.mdc`). A `Long` constant named `*_MS` may stay; convert it at the call.
